{"surah": 92, "verses": [{"ayah": 1, "text": "وَٱلَّيْلِ إِذَا يَغْشَىٰ ", "translation": "By the night when it covers[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلَّيْلِ", "translit": "ِlْyَّlٱَw", "root": "ليل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَغْشَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَhsْغَy", "root": "غشو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 2, "text": "وَٱلنَّهَارِ إِذَا تَجَلَّىٰ ", "translation": "And [by] the day when it appears", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱلنَّهَارِ", "translit": "ِrāَhَّnlٱَw", "root": "نهر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَجَلَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّlَjgَt", "root": "جلو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 3, "text": "وَمَا خَلَقَ ٱلذَّكَرَ وَٱلْأُنثَىٰٓ ", "translation": "And [by] He who created the male and female,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "خَلَقَ", "translit": "َqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلذَّكَرَ", "translit": "َrَkَّhdlٱ", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٱلْأُنثَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَhtnُaʾْlٱَw", "root": "انث"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 4, "text": "إِنَّ سَعْيَكُمْ لَشَتَّىٰ ", "translation": "Indeed, your efforts are diverse.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "سَعْيَكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkَyْʿعَs", "root": "سعي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَشَتَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّtَhsَl", "root": "شتت"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 5, "text": "فَأَمَّا مَنْ أَعْطَىٰ وَٱتَّقَىٰ ", "translation": "As for he who gives and fears Allāh", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَaʾَf", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَنْ", "translit": "ْnَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "أَعْطَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَṭْʿعَaʾ", "root": "عطو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٱتَّقَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَqَّtٱَw", "root": "وقي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 6, "text": "وَصَدَّقَ بِٱلْحُسْنَىٰ ", "translation": "And believes in the best [reward].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَصَدَّقَ", "translit": "َqَّdَṣَw", "root": "صدق"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِٱلْحُسْنَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَnْsُḥْlٱِb", "root": "حسن"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 7, "text": "فَسَنُيَسِّرُهُۥ لِلْيُسْرَىٰ ", "translation": "We will ease him toward ease.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَسَنُيَسِّرُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُrِّsَyُnَsَf", "root": "يسر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِلْيُسْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْsُyْlِl", "root": "يسر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 8, "text": "وَأَمَّا مَنۢ بَخِلَ وَٱسْتَغْنَىٰ ", "translation": "But as for he who withholds and considers himself free of need", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأَمَّا", "translit": "āَّmَaʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَنۢ", "translit": "ۢnَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بَخِلَ", "translit": "َlِhkَb", "root": "بخل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٱسْتَغْنَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَnْغَtْsٱَw", "root": "غني"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 9, "text": "وَكَذَّبَ بِٱلْحُسْنَىٰ ", "translation": "And denies the best [reward],", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكَذَّبَ", "translit": "َbَّhdَkَw", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِٱلْحُسْنَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَnْsُḥْlٱِb", "root": "حسن"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 10, "text": "فَسَنُيَسِّرُهُۥ لِلْعُسْرَىٰ ", "translation": "We will ease him toward difficulty.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَسَنُيَسِّرُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُrِّsَyُnَsَf", "root": "يسر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِلْعُسْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْsُʿعْlِl", "root": "عسر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 11, "text": "وَمَا يُغْنِى عَنْهُ مَالُهُۥٓ إِذَا تَرَدَّىٰٓ ", "translation": "And what will his wealth avail him when he falls?[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يُغْنِى", "translit": "àِnْغُy", "root": "غني"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عَنْهُ", "translit": "ُhْnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَالُهُۥٓ", "translit": "ٓۥُhُlāَm", "root": "مول"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِذَا", "translit": "āَhdِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تَرَدَّىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَّdَrَt", "root": "ردي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 12, "text": "إِنَّ عَلَيْنَا لَلْهُدَىٰ ", "translation": "Indeed, [incumbent] upon Us is guidance.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَلْهُدَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَdُhْlَl", "root": "هدي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 13, "text": "وَإِنَّ لَنَا لَلْءَاخِرَةَ وَٱلْأُولَىٰ ", "translation": "And indeed, to us belongs the Hereafter and the first [life].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَإِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَنَا", "translit": "āَnَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لَلْءَاخِرَةَ", "translit": "َẗَrِhkāَءْlَl", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٱلْأُولَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlwُaʾْlٱَw", "root": "اول"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 14, "text": "فَأَنذَرْتُكُمْ نَارًا تَلَظَّىٰ ", "translation": "So I have warned you of a Fire which is blazing.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَأَنذَرْتُكُمْ", "translit": "ْmُkُtْrَhdnَaʾَf", "root": "نذر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نَارًا", "translit": "āًrāَn", "root": "نور"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَلَظَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّẓَlَt", "root": "لظي"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 15, "text": "لَا يَصْلَىٰهَآ إِلَّا ٱلْأَشْقَى ", "translation": "None will [enter to] burn therein except the most wretched one", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَصْلَىٰهَآ", "translit": "ٓāَhٰàَlْṣَy", "root": "صلي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْأَشْقَى", "translit": "àَqْhsَaʾْlٱ", "root": "شقو"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 16, "text": "ٱلَّذِى كَذَّبَ وَتَوَلَّىٰ ", "translation": "Who had denied and turned away.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كَذَّبَ", "translit": "َbَّhdَk", "root": "كذب"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَتَوَلَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّlَwَtَw", "root": "ولي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 17, "text": "وَسَيُجَنَّبُهَا ٱلْأَتْقَى ", "translation": "But the righteous one will avoid it –", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَسَيُجَنَّبُهَا", "translit": "āَhُbَّnَjgُyَsَw", "root": "جنب"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْأَتْقَى", "translit": "àَqْtَaʾْlٱ", "root": "وقي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 18, "text": "ٱلَّذِى يُؤْتِى مَالَهُۥ يَتَزَكَّىٰ ", "translation": "[He] who gives [from] his wealth to purify himself", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يُؤْتِى", "translit": "àِtْwʾُy", "root": "اتي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَالَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَlāَm", "root": "مول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَتَزَكَّىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَّkَzَtَy", "root": "زكو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 19, "text": "وَمَا لِأَحَدٍ عِندَهُۥ مِن نِّعْمَةٍ تُجْزَىٰٓ ", "translation": "And not [giving] for anyone who has [done him] a favor to be rewarded[1] –", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِأَحَدٍ", "translit": "ٍdَḥَaʾِl", "root": "احد"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "عِندَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَdnِʿع", "root": "عند"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "نِّعْمَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَmْʿعِّn", "root": "نعم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "تُجْزَىٰٓ", "translit": "ٰٓàَzْjgُt", "root": "جزي"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 20, "text": "إِلَّا ٱبْتِغَآءَ وَجْهِ رَبِّهِ ٱلْأَعْلَىٰ ", "translation": "But only seeking the face [i.e., acceptance] of his Lord, Most High.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِلَّا", "translit": "āَّlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱبْتِغَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَغِtْbٱ", "root": "بغي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "وَجْهِ", "translit": "ِhْjgَw", "root": "وجه"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "رَبِّهِ", "translit": "ِhِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْأَعْلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlْʿعَaʾْlٱ", "root": "علو"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 21, "text": "وَلَسَوْفَ يَرْضَىٰ ", "translation": "And he is going to be satisfied.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَسَوْفَ", "translit": "َfْwَsَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَرْضَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَḍْrَy", "root": "رضو"}], "hasIhya": false}], "tafsir": {"5": [{"arabic": "بالترغيب بالخير وتيسير الأمر عليه وإليه الإشارة بقوله تعالى فأما من أعطى واتقى وصدق بالحسنى فسنيسره لليسرى وفي مثل هذا القلب يشرق نور المصباح من مكشاة الربوبية حتى لا يخفي فيه الشرك الخفي الذي هو أخفى من دبيب النملة السوداء في الليلة الظلماء فلا يخفى على هذا النور خافية ولا يروج عليه شيء من مكايد الشيطان بل يقف الشيطان ويوحي زخرف القول غرورا فلا يلتفت إليه وهذا القلب بعد طهارته من المهلكات يصير على القرب معمورا بالمنجيات التي سنذكرها من الشكر والصبر والخوف والرجاء والفقر والزهد والمحبة والرضا والشوق والتوكل والتفكر والمحاسبة وغير ذلك وهو القلب", "english": "Know that the heart, as we have mentioned, is surrounded by the attributes we have described, and the traces and states pour into it from the gateways we have set forth. It is as if it were a target perpetually struck from every side. When something afflicts it, it is affected by it; then it is struck from another side by that which opposes it, and so its state changes. If the devil descends upon it and calls it to passion (hawā), the angel descends upon it and turns it away from that. If one devil drags it toward evil, another devil drags it toward something else; and if one angel draws it toward good, another draws it toward something else. Thus at times it is contested between two angels, at times between two devils, and at times between an angel and a devil — it is never, ever left neglected. To this points the saying of the Exalted: {وَنُقَلِّبُ أَفْئِدَتَهُمْ وَأَبْصَارَهُمْ} (And We turn their hearts and their visions) {«الأنعام/110»}. And because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was privy to the wondrous handiwork of Allah the Exalted in the marvels of the heart and its overturning, he used to swear by it, saying: \"No, by the Turner of the hearts.\" (1)", "book_title": "Patience and Gratitude", "section_title_ar": "Section 610", "section_title_en": "The Wonders of the Heart: Its States of Stability, Inclination, and Fluctuation Between the Two Attracting Forces", "section_index": 610, "anchors": "- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn a thing over, to revolve it, to divert it from one face to another; the heart is so named for its constant turning and fluctuation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart's perpetual overturning is its very essence; its states are never fixed, and its rectitude or corruption follows its turning.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" — physical overturning. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — one who turns matters over and manages them; the heart's fluctuation between the two attractants is a real, continuous overturning.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين الجار والمجرور (Prepositional Interposition) — The periodic structure of the opening sentence, with its cascading circumstantial clauses, requires careful tracking of the governing verb and its dependent states to preserve the causal sequence of the heart's affliction and response.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Patience and Gratitude'", "badge": "Insight"}], "14": [{"arabic": "للكافرين وقال تعالى فأنذرتكم نارا تلظى لا يصلاها إلا الأشقى الذي كذب وتولى وقال عز وجل وإن ربك لذوا مغفرة للناس على ظلمهم ويقال أن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم لم يزل يسأل في أمته حتى قيل له أما ترضى وقد أنزلت عليك هذه الآية وإن ربك لذو مغفرة للناس ظلمهم (2) وفي تفسير قوله تعالى {ولسوف يعطيك ربك فترضى} قال لا يرضى محمد وواحد من أمته في النار وكان أبو جعفر محمد بن علي يقول أنتم أهل العراق تقولون أرجى آية في كتاب الله عز وجل قوله {قل يا عبادي الذين أسرفوا على أنفسهم لا تقنطوا من رحمة الله} الآية ونحن أهل البيت نقول أرجى آية في كتاب الله تعا", "english": "So it was said to him: \"And what is in it of hope?\" He said: \"This world, all of it, is little, and a man's provision from it is little, and his religion is little in comparison to his provision. So look at how God Most High sent down in it the longest verse, to guide His servant to the path of caution in preserving his wealth — so how could He not preserve his religion, which has no substitute for him?\"", "book_title": "Fear and Hope", "section_title_ar": "Section 1033", "section_title_en": "The Book of Hope (Kitāb al-Rajā') — Section 1033: The Second Art — Surveying the Verses and Reports Concerning Hope", "section_index": 1033, "anchors": "- Root: وجود (w-j-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of \"finding\" and \"existence\"; here in the sense of what is present and available.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Existence as that which is found and realized; in theological context, the given order of creation.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) sense of \"being found\"; metaphorical (majāz) extension to the entirety of created things.\n- Root: رجا (r-j-w)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To hope, to expect with desire; the opposite of despair (ya's).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hope as a theological virtue — the heart's expectation of divine mercy, balanced against fear (khawf).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: expectation of a desired outcome; metaphorical: the soul's reliance on divine generosity.\n- Root: غفر (gh-f-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To cover, to shield, to pardon; the root implies veiling the fault.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Divine forgiveness as the covering of sins and the effacing of their consequence.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: covering; metaphorical: God's remission of punishment.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — used to emphasize exclusive attributes of divine mercy and forgiveness.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Fear and Hope'", "badge": "Insight"}], "15": [{"arabic": "مكذبا ولقوله تعالى {لا يصلاها إلا الأشقى الذي كذب وتولى} وهذا حصر وإثبات ونفي ولقوله تعالى {من جاء بالحسنة فله خير منها وهم من فزع يومئذ آمنون} فالإيمان رأس الحسنات ولقوله تعالى {والله يحب المحسنين} وقال تعالى {إنا لا نضيع أجر من أحسن عملا} ولا حجة لهم في ذلك فإنه حيث ذكر الإيمان في هذه الآيات أريد به الإيمان مع العمل إذ بينا أن الإيمان قد يطلق ويراد به الإسلام وهو الموافقة بالقلب والقول والعمل ودليل هذا التأويل أخبار كثيرة في معاقبة العاصين ومقادير العقاب وقوله صلى الله عليه وسلم يخرج من النار من كان في قلب", "english": "He was asked, \"Which deed is most excellent?\" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Islam.\" It was then asked, \"Which aspect of Islam is most excellent?\" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: \"Faith (Īmān).\" This is evidence for both the distinction (ikhtilāf) between the two terms and their overlap (tadākhul). This usage is the most consistent with the conventions of the language, because faith is a deed among deeds, and it is the most excellent of them. Islam is submission (taslīm), whether by the heart, by the tongue, or by the limbs. The most excellent of these is that which is by the heart, which is the assent (taṣdīq) that is called faith. Using the two terms in a way that indicates distinction, overlap, or even complete synonymy (tarāduf) is all within the bounds of metaphorical usage (tajawwuz) in the language.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 99", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 99: On the Distinction, Overlap, and Synonymy of Faith (Īmān) and Submission (Islām), and Their Worldly and Otherworldly Judgments", "section_index": 99, "anchors": "- Root: ايم (A-Y-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of remaining unmarried, or a state of being without a spouse; extended metaphorically to describe one who is solitary or without support.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الأيامى: جمع أيم، وهي المرأة التي لا بعل لها... والحرب مأيمة، أي: يفرق بين الزوج والزوجة.\" The root carries the sense of separation or a state of lacking union, which informs the theological discussion of what constitutes the essential bond of faith.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage denotes the state of being unmarried; metaphorical (Majaz) usage extends to anything that separates or leaves one bereft. This distinction between literal and metaphorical application is central to the chapter's method.\n- Root: قلب (Q-L-B)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه... والانقلاب: الانصراف.\" The heart (qalb) is so named for its constant fluctuation and turning. In theological terms, it is the seat of taṣdīq (assent), the immaterial faculty of cognition and conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is physical turning; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"قلب الأمور\" — to deliberate, scrutinize, and manage affairs. This supports the distinction between the heart's internal act of assent and the external acts of the limbs.\n- Root: عمل (ʿ-A-M-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Any deed or action performed with purpose and intent.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العمل: كل فعل يكون من الحيوان بقصد، فهو أخص من الفعل.\" Work is more specific than mere action, as it implies deliberate, intentional agency. It is used for both righteous and sinful deeds, and is the external manifestation of internal conviction.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage refers to physical labor or deeds. The chapter explores whether \"work\" is an integral part of the reality of faith or a separate, consequent requirement.\n- Root: صدق (S-D-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To be truthful, to speak the truth, to verify.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الصدق والكذب أصلهما في القول... ولا يكونان بالقصد الأول إلا في القول.\" Truth and falsehood are primarily properties of speech and reports. Taṣdīq (assent) is the heart's affirmation of the truth of a report, which is the core reality of Īmān.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah) usage is truthful speech; metaphorical (Majaz) usage is \"صادق الحملة\" — one who is true and firm in battle or resolve. This shows that taṣdīq is not merely verbal but a firm, internal resolution.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication). This rule governs how restriction (ḥaṣr) is achieved in Arabic, often through particles like \"innamā\" or through contextual specification (takhṣīṣ). The chapter's core argument hinges on how terms like Īmān and Islām are semantically restricted, expanded, or made synonymous through contextual usage (majāz), requiring precise parsing of the predicate and its scope.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 330, 1597], [2, 1597, 2489], [3, 2489, 4080]], "92:1": [[1, 330, 1597], [2, 1597, 2489], [3, 2489, 4080]], "2": [[1, 200, 2538], [2, 2538, 3646], [3, 3646, 5400]], "92:2": [[1, 200, 2538], [2, 2538, 3646], [3, 3646, 5400]], "3": [[1, 210, 1427], [2, 1427, 2095], [3, 2095, 3147], [4, 3147, 5990]], "92:3": [[1, 210, 1427], [2, 1427, 2095], [3, 2095, 3147], [4, 3147, 5990]], "4": [[1, 40, 1012], [2, 1012, 2942], [3, 2942, 5260]], "92:4": [[1, 40, 1012], [2, 1012, 2942], [3, 2942, 5260]], "5": [[1, 200, 1818], [2, 1818, 2274], [3, 2274, 3773], [4, 3773, 6020]], "92:5": [[1, 200, 1818], [2, 1818, 2274], [3, 2274, 3773], [4, 3773, 6020]], "6": [[1, 200, 1637], [2, 1637, 3750]], "92:6": [[1, 200, 1637], [2, 1637, 3750]], "7": [[1, 200, 2514], [2, 2514, 5160]], "92:7": [[1, 200, 2514], [2, 2514, 5160]], "8": [[1, 10, 2257], [2, 2257, 2786], [3, 2